character_decomposition[2]{character,pinyin,meaning_en,meaning_es,radical,radical_meaning_en,meaning_component,sound_component,stroke_count,composition_type,etymology_and_mnemonic,visual_mnemonic_story_en,visual_mnemonic_story_es,tone_mnemonic_anchor,cangjie,traditional}:
  点 | diǎn | drop; dot; touch; spot; light up | punto; gota; tocar levemente; encender | 灬 | fire | 黑 (black/soot) + 灬 (fire) | 占 (zhàn, divine / phonetic) | 9 | 上下结构 | Ancient character showing soot marks from a lamp flame 灬; later simplified to touching or touching lightly. | A spark of flame (灬) touches the wick to place a small dot of light on the table (点). | Una chispa de fuego (灬) roza la mecha depositando una pizca de luz sobre la mesa (点). | 214 | YRBO | 點
  心 | xin | heart; core; mind; affection | corazón; centro; mente; afecto | 心 | heart | 心 (pictogram of the human heart) | 心 (xīn) | 4 | 单一结构 | Ancient oracle bone pictogram of the human heart showing ventricles and aorta chambers. | The three drops surround the curved ventricle chamber to depict the living human heart (心). | Las tres gotas rodean la cavidad central para representar el corazón humano palpitante (心). | 55 | P | ""

mnemonics_and_roots:
  composite_memory_hook_en: A light snack touches (点) and comforts the stomach and heart (心), hence pastry (点心).
  composite_memory_hook_es: Un bocado ligero que toca (点) y conforta el corazón (心) se convierte en merienda (点心).
  cultural_and_cognitive_roots: Folklore traces 'diǎnxīn' to the Tang/Song dynasties, signifying eating a tiny bite just enough to 'touch the heart' (点点心意) before hunger strikes.
  tone_sandhi_and_pitch_modulation: Dipping tone followed by neutral tone: 'diǎn xin' (214-2). Deliver 'diǎn' with a full 3rd tone and drop 'xin' softly into an unstressed neutral tone.

traps_and_cognates:
  look_alikes[4]{character,pinyin,meaning_en,distinction_note}:
    占 | zhàn | occupy; divine | Lacks the four bottom fire dots 灬 of 点
    店 | diàn | shop; store | Has shelter radical 广 instead of bottom fire dots
    必 | bì | must; surely | Has a long diagonal slashing stroke through 心
    忒 | tuī | excessive | Has 弋 on the upper right of 心
  etymological_cognates[4]{character,pinyin,gloss_en}:
    糕 | gāo | steamed cake of rice/flour
    饼 | bǐng | round flat biscuit or pastry
    馅 | xiàn | pastry stuffing or filling
    馍 | mó | steamed flour bun or bread

semantic_boundary_contrasts:
  core_nuance: 点心 refers to baked, steamed, or fried pastries and snacks eaten between formal meals.
  contrasts[3]{id,compared_word,pinyin,analysis}:
    1 | 点心 vs. 零食 | língshí | 零食 encompasses all processed casual snacks (candies, chips, nuts) eaten casually, while 点心 specifically refers to cooked, baked, or culinary pastries.
    2 | 点心 vs. 甜点 | tiándiǎn | 甜点 is strictly sweet desserts served at the conclusion of meals, whereas 点心 includes both savory meat buns and sweet pastries.
    3 | 点心 vs. 小吃 | xiǎochī | 小吃 covers regional street foods (noodles, tofu, skewers), while 点心 leans toward refined flour/rice pastries and teahouse refreshments.

extended_reading_passages[2]{id,hanzi,pinyin,en,es}:
  #1 | 午后暖阳斜照在雕花木桌上，一壶清冽的龙井配上两块软糯的桂花点心，勾勒出岁月静好的江南午后。那甜而不腻的豆沙馅料入口即化，在唇齿间弥散开淡淡的花香，将所有的奔波劳碌都化作了舌尖上的温存。 | Wǔhòu nuǎnyáng xiézhào zài diāohuā mùzhuō shàng, yì hú qīngliè de Lóngjǐng pèishàng liǎng kuài ruǎnnuò de guìhuā diǎnxin, gōulè chū suìyuè-jìnghǎo de Jiāngnán wǔhòu. Nà tián ér bú nì de dòushā xiànliào rùkǒu-jíhuà, zài chúnchǐ jiān mísàn kāi dàndàn de huāxiāng, jiāng suǒyǒu de bēnbō-láolù dōu huàzuò le shéjiān shàng de wēncún. | Warm afternoon sun slants across the carved table, where Longjing tea and sweet osmanthus pastries outline southern tranquility. The delicate bean paste melts on the tongue, dispersing floral fragrance and melting daily weariness. | El sol vespertino baña la mesa de madera labrada; una tetera de té Longjing y pastelitos de osmanto dibujan la quietud del sur. El dulce relleno se deshace en el paladar, disipando las fatigas del día con su sutil aroma floral.
  #2 | 传统中式点心不仅是美味的物质享受，更承载着中华民族深厚的节庆民俗与礼仪文化。从八月十五象征阖家团圆的月饼，到端午时节包裹青竹清香的粽子，每一道手工点心的纹样与馅料，都寄托着人们对美好生活的永恒期盼。 | Chuántǒng zhōngshì diǎnxin bùjǐn shì měiwèi de wùzhì xiǎngshòu, gèng chéngzài zhe Zhōnghuá mínzú shēnhòu de jiéqìng mínsú yǔ lǐyí wénhuà. Cóng bāyuè shíwǔ xiàngzhēng héjiā-tuányuán de yuèbǐng, dào Duānwǔ shíjié bāoguǒ qīngzhú qīngxiāng de zòngzi, měi yí dào shǒugōng diǎnxin de wényàng yǔ xiànliào, dōu jìtuō zhe rénmen duì měihǎo shēnghuó de yǒnghéng qīpàn. | Traditional pastries offer not only culinary pleasure, but embody festive customs and etiquette. From Mid-Autumn mooncakes symbolizing reunion to Dragon Boat dumplings wrapped in bamboo, every pattern carries hopes for a good life. | La repostería china tradicional encarna un rico legado festivo. Desde los pasteles de luna que celebran la unión familiar hasta los tamales de bambú en el festival del dragón, cada manjar artesano alberga los mejores deseos de bienestar.
